## 5. Retrieving sequence data in code

The source builds a SeqinR helper that searches remote databases. A browser lesson should not make an unrecorded network request on every run. Preparation retrieves the exact external records once; analysis then stays local and repeatable.

```biolang
let prepared_records = table({
    accession: [dengue_record.id, leprae_record.id],
    description: [dengue_record.description, leprae_record.description],
    nucleotides: [dengue_record.length, leprae_record.length]
})
prepared_records
```

## 6. Writing and reading FASTA

The source writes the retrieved sequence to `den1.fasta` and reads it back. In Studio the prepared FASTA already is that saved interchange file. Native BioLang can use `write_fasta(records, path)`; the browser uses its download controls instead of writing arbitrary computer paths.

The useful round-trip check is that the parsed record still has the intended header and length.

```biolang
{
    fasta_header: ">" + dengue_record.id + " " + dengue_record.description,
    first_50: subseq(dengue_record.seq, 0, 50),
    first_50_length: len(subseq(dengue_record.seq, 0, 50))
}
```

## 7. Work with a DNA value

`read_fasta()` returns a table because one FASTA file may contain many records. Selecting the first row's `seq` field gives a typed DNA value. BioLang then prevents protein-only operations being accidentally applied to DNA.

```biolang
let dengue = dengue_record.seq
let leprae = leprae_record.seq

{
    dengue_type: type(dengue),
    first_50: subseq(dengue, 0, 50),
    length: len(dengue)
}
```

BioLang's `subseq(sequence, start, end)` uses a zero-based start and an end that is not included. Therefore `subseq(dengue, 0, 50)` contains exactly 50 bases. The biological position is one larger than the zero-based program index.

## 8. Length of a DNA sequence

```biolang
len(dengue)
```

**Result:** 10,735 nucleotides, matching the source chapter.

## 9. Base composition

Base composition answers “how many A, C, G, and T letters occur?” It is a first quality check and a compact description of a genome, not an explanation of why the composition arose.

```biolang
let dengue_bases = base_counts(dengue)
table({
    base: ["A", "C", "G", "T"],
    count: [dengue_bases.A, dengue_bases.C, dengue_bases.G, dengue_bases.T]
})
```

## 10. GC content

GC content is the fraction `(G + C) / sequence length`. A fraction of `0.4667` and a percentage of `46.67%` describe the same result. Always state which scale you report.

```biolang
let dengue_gc = gc_content(dengue)
{
    fraction: dengue_gc,
    percent: 100 * dengue_gc,
    hand_check: 1.0 * (dengue_bases.G + dengue_bases.C) / dengue_bases.total
}
```

If a sequence contains ambiguous symbols, decide whether they belong in the denominator. Neither choice is universally right: including them describes the whole assembled record; excluding them describes only confidently called A/C/G/T positions.

## 11. DNA words and direction

A k-mer is a word of length `k`. Adjacent words overlap: `ATG` contains the 2-mers `AT` and `TG`. The table below counts directional words exactly as the original chapter does.

```biolang
fn selected_dinucleotide_counts(seq) {
    let positions = kmer_index(seq, 2)
    {CC: len(positions.CC), CG: len(positions.CG), GC: len(positions.GC)}
}

let dengue_words = kmer_index(dengue, 2)
table({
    word: ["AA", "AC", "AG", "AT", "CA", "CC", "CG", "CT", "GA", "GC", "GG", "GT", "TA", "TC", "TG", "TT"],
    count: [
        len(dengue_words.AA), len(dengue_words.AC), len(dengue_words.AG), len(dengue_words.AT),
        len(dengue_words.CA), len(dengue_words.CC), len(dengue_words.CG), len(dengue_words.CT),
        len(dengue_words.GA), len(dengue_words.GC), len(dengue_words.GG), len(dengue_words.GT),
        len(dengue_words.TA), len(dengue_words.TC), len(dengue_words.TG), len(dengue_words.TT)
    ]
})
```

`kmer_count()` is excellent for many genomics workflows, but its default canonical representation groups a word with its reverse complement. This lesson uses `kmer_index()` because `CG` and `GC` must remain separate to answer the exercises exactly.

# Exercises and worked solutions

Try each question before expanding or running its solution step.

## Exercise 1 · Last twenty Dengue nucleotides

What are the last 20 nucleotides of `NC_001477.1`?

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 1 · Last twenty nucleotides" -->

The final 20 bases start at `length - 20`. Omitting the end argument continues to the end of the sequence.

```biolang
let exercise_1 = subseq(dengue, len(dengue) - 20)
exercise_1
```

**Answer:** `CTGTTGAATCAACAGGTTCT`.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## Exercise 2 · *M. leprae* genome length

How many nucleotides are in `NC_002677.1`?

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 2 · Genome length" -->

```biolang
let exercise_2 = len(leprae)
exercise_2
```

**Answer:** 3,268,203 nucleotides.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## Exercise 3 · Every base symbol

Count A, C, G, T, and any ambiguous symbols in the *M. leprae* record.

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 3 · Base composition" -->

```biolang
let leprae_bases = base_counts(leprae)
table({
    symbol: ["A", "C", "G", "T", "N"],
    count: [leprae_bases.A, leprae_bases.C, leprae_bases.G, leprae_bases.T, leprae_bases.N]
})
```

**Answer:** A 687,041; C 938,713; G 950,202; T 692,247; N 0. The four counts sum to the full sequence length, so this record contains no ambiguous symbols.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## Exercise 4 · GC with two denominators

Calculate GC content (i) using every position and (ii) after excluding non-A/C/G/T positions.

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 4 · GC denominators" -->

```biolang
let called_bases = leprae_bases.A + leprae_bases.C + leprae_bases.G + leprae_bases.T
let gc_all_positions = 1.0 * (leprae_bases.G + leprae_bases.C) / leprae_bases.total
let gc_called_positions = 1.0 * (leprae_bases.G + leprae_bases.C) / called_bases
{
    including_ambiguous_positions: gc_all_positions,
    excluding_ambiguous_positions: gc_called_positions,
    difference: gc_called_positions - gc_all_positions
}
```

**Answer:** both fractions are `0.5779674641` (about 57.80%) because there are no ambiguous positions in this versioned record.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## Exercise 5 · Complement composition

Count the four bases after complementing the genome. Predict the result before running it.

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 5 · Complement counts" -->

```biolang
let complement_bases = base_counts(complement(leprae))
table({
    base: ["A", "C", "G", "T"],
    original: [leprae_bases.A, leprae_bases.C, leprae_bases.G, leprae_bases.T],
    complement: [complement_bases.A, complement_bases.C, complement_bases.G, complement_bases.T]
})
```

**Answer:** A 692,247; C 950,202; G 938,713; T 687,041. Complementing swaps A with T and C with G; it does not reverse their order.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## Exercise 6 · Three directional dinucleotides

How often do `CC`, `CG`, and `GC` occur in the full *M. leprae* genome?

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 6 · Full-genome words" -->

```biolang
let full_dinucleotides = selected_dinucleotide_counts(leprae)
full_dinucleotides
```

**Answer:** CC 236,971; CG 306,986; GC 293,261.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## Exercise 7 · First and last 1,000 bases

Count the same words in the first and last 1,000 nucleotides. Also prove that each selected region has length 1,000.

<!-- bl:step title="Solution 7 · Compare the genome ends" -->

```biolang
let leprae_first_1000 = subseq(leprae, 0, 1000)
let leprae_last_1000 = subseq(leprae, len(leprae) - 1000)
let first_counts = selected_dinucleotide_counts(leprae_first_1000)
let last_counts = selected_dinucleotide_counts(leprae_last_1000)

table({
    region: ["first 1,000", "last 1,000"],
    length: [len(leprae_first_1000), len(leprae_last_1000)],
    CC: [first_counts.CC, last_counts.CC],
    CG: [first_counts.CG, last_counts.CG],
    GC: [first_counts.GC, last_counts.GC]
})
```

**Answer:** first 1,000: CC 82, CG 92, GC 63. Last 1,000: CC 81, CG 87, GC 75. Each region contains 999 overlapping dinucleotide positions.

<!-- /bl:step -->

## 12. Summary

You can now read versioned FASTA records, distinguish record metadata from sequence data, select exact subsequences, count bases, calculate GC content with an explicit denominator, complement DNA, and count directional overlapping words. The next notebook turns one whole-genome number into a plot showing how composition changes along a genome.

